<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940)</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1940 CE · capital Kaunas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Interwar independent Lithuania, ruled from Kaunas because of the loss of Vilnius, and later under the authoritarian presidency of Antanas Smetona.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 16 February 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Antanas Smetona</a>, declared independence at a session presided over by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonas-basanavicius">Jonas Basanavicius</a>. After a brief attempt at the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lithuania-1918">Kingdom of Lithuania</a> (1918), it fought Soviet, Bermontian and Polish forces. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> seized Vilnius in 1920, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> became the temporary capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution of 1922 created a parliamentary republic under presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandras-stulginskis">Aleksandras Stulginskis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazys-grinius">Kazys Grinius</a>. Land reform broke up great estates, and dairy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> exports grew. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipeda</a> in 1923. In December 1926 a military coup restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Smetona</a> as president, and he ruled in an authoritarian, nationalist style until 1940.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1939 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipeda</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany</a> and regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> from the USSR. In June 1940 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied the country, and a staged parliament proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lithuanian-soviet-socialist-republic">Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Smetona</a> fled abroad.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940.jpg" alt="Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940)" width="320" height="273"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Jansz van Santen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B10_039-MAGNI_DVCATVS_LITHVANIAE,_et_Regionum_Adiacentium_exacta_Descriptio_....jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1940 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaunas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antanas Smetona, Aleksandras Stulginskis, Kazys Grinius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lithuanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First Republic of Lithuania, Interwar Lithuania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1929&amp;focus=republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940" aria-label="Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5206 300 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5526 556L5546 556M5526 556L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5546 556h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5526 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5526 556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kaunas. <a href="/atlas?year=1929&amp;focus=republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
